<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hakor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 380 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Achoris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hakor, called Achoris by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, was the strongest king of the Twenty-ninth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning for about thirteen years in the 390s and 380s BCE. He allied with Evagoras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, hired Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chabrias">Chabrias</a> and repelled a Persian attack around 385 to 383 BCE. He built widely in Egypt, though many of his works were later reinscribed by his successors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hakor.jpg" alt="Hakor" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flinders Petrie (1853-1942) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Achoris_Petrie_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hakor, also called Hagar and known in Greek as Achoris or Hakoris, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 29th Dynasty. His accession and his relationship with his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-i">Nepherites I</a> were long debated. After Nepherites died a dynastic struggle seems to have followed, in which Hakor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psammuthes">Psammuthes</a> and possibly a figure called Muthis, mentioned only in Eusebius' epitome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>, claimed the throne. In 1986 John D. Ray suggested that Hakor was Nepherites' heir, deposed in his second year by Psammuthes and restored a year later, and that he dated his reign from his first coronation as though the gap had never occurred. Alan B. Lloyd and Toby Wilkinson accept this view.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Once re-established, Hakor made considerable efforts to affirm his legitimacy, stressing his real or fictional descent from Nepherites. His building was extensive and he restored many monuments of earlier kings. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> he finished the chapel for the sacred barque of Amun-Ra near the first pylon, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psammuthes">Psammuthes</a> or possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-i">Nepherites I</a>, and he possibly began a temple complex in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> that was later developed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-ii">Nectanebo II</a>. His building is attested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinet-habu">Medinet Habu</a>, El-Kab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tod-egypt">El-Tod</a>, Medamud and Elephantine, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-hibis">Temple of Hibis</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharga-oasis">Kharga Oasis</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>.</p><p>Hakor continued Nepherites' foreign policy and was allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evagoras-i">Evagoras I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>. After the peace of Antalcidas in 387 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and Cyprus remained the only opponents of Artaxerxes II. It seems the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> first attacked Egypt in 385 BCE and, after three years of war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> defeated them. In 381 BCE Hakor sent Evagoras aid, money and 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>, but when Evagoras came to Egypt in 380 BCE to beg for more he was sent back with only some money. Hakor then joined a short-lived alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and with Glos, and took the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chabrias">Chabrias</a> into his service.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hakor was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-ii">Nepherites II</a>, who kept the throne for only four months before an army general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Sebennytos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a>, overthrew him. Nectanebo I called Hakor an usurper shortly after his death, though it has been suggested that the two were relatives, possibly both related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepherites-i">Nepherites I</a> but rivals. Hakor's tomb has not been found. Only one funerary object can be linked to him, an ushabti inscribed with his name found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a>, which may indicate that his tomb was looted in antiquity, though this is unproven.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hakor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dynasty's strongest king, <b>Hakor</b> (Achoris), ruled for about thirteen years.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-ninth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
